          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "8bf0bb08-c7e3-37be-9407-994db8e810c2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code snippet, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that is stored in an environment block associated with the process. If the environment variable is changed, the pointer returned by `std::getenv` may become invalid.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` function as it is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that are thread-safe and do not have the same vulnerabilities. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* log_level;\nsize_t requiredSize;\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, \"HIPDNN_LOG_LEVEL\");\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    printf(\"HIPDNN_LOG_LEVEL doesn't exist!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\nlog_level = (char*) malloc(requiredSize * sizeof(char));\nif (!log_level)\n{\n    printf(\"Failed to allocate memory!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, log_level, requiredSize, \"HIPDNN_LOG_LEVEL\");\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: For `std::getenv` function.\n- `<cstdio>`: For `printf` function.\n- `<stdlib.h>`: For `exit` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "87ba04a6-85be-315b-99f7-49960e069b79",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function (`strncpy`) where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard, as `strncpy` does not support overlapping memory regions. The analysis detected a feasible dataflow path where the destination could be used as the source, increasing the risk. There are no null pointer risks for either source or destination, and the code does explicitly NUL-terminate the destination after the copy, but this does not mitigate the undefined behavior risk from overlapping buffers. The source and destination are both identifiers, and the overlap was determined via dataflow analysis, which further supports the risk assessment.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying between buffers that may overlap, use a function that is safe for overlapping memory regions. In C/C++, `memmove` is designed for this purpose. Replace the use of `strncpy` with `memmove` if the data being copied may not be null-terminated, or ensure that the source and destination do not overlap before calling `strncpy`.\n\nFor example, if you must copy up to `max_size - 1` bytes and NUL-terminate:\n\n```cpp\n// Replace this:\n// std::strncpy(destination, source, max_size - 1);\n\n// With this:\nmemmove(destination, source, max_size - 1);\ndestination[max_size - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIf you require string semantics (stop at NUL), you may need to compute the length to copy first, or ensure that the source and destination do not overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "35fb0073-6952-35c9-9f40-42c41e508370",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the function `std::getenv` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that is stored in an environment block. The problem is that the returned string can be overwritten by subsequent calls to `std::getenv`, `std::setenv`, or `std::putenv`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` function. Instead, use secure alternatives that are thread-safe and do not have the risk of being overwritten. If you must use environment variables, consider copying the returned string to a local buffer immediately after calling `std::getenv`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* log_file_path_temp = std::getenv(\"HIPDNN_LOG_FILE\");\nif (log_file_path_temp != nullptr) {\n    std::string log_file_path = log_file_path_temp;\n    // Use log_file_path\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: For `std::getenv` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "08d83bd6-2f53-38ea-a199-365fbe5b4150",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code snippet, the function `std::getenv` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that represents the value for the specified environment variable. An attacker could potentially modify this environment variable, leading to unexpected behavior in the program.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, consider using a method that does not involve environment variables to set the log level. If you must use environment variables, ensure that your program validates and sanitizes the input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Instead of using std::getenv, consider using a configuration file or command line arguments to set the log level.\n// If you must use an environment variable, validate and sanitize the input.\n\nconst char* log_level_env = std::getenv(\"HIPDNN_LOG_LEVEL\");\n\nif (log_level_env != nullptr) {\n    std::string log_level = log_level_env;\n    // Validate and sanitize log_level before using it.\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This is a standard library in C++ that provides general purpose functions including process control, dynamic memory allocation, random numbers, and others.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Buffer_overflow_attack)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
